# Never Name Network / Sub-Vendors — NON-NEGOTIABLE

## 🚨 THE RULE — NEVER NAME PARTNERS TO PROSPECTS. EVER.

**If you tell a prospect who DDI uses for fulfillment, they WILL contact that partner directly and cut DDI out. This is not theoretical — it happens.**

When generating **any outbound document or correspondence** intended for external recipients (buyers, MCOs, government, suppliers, subcontractors in discovery, portal posts, cold emails, capability statements, proposals, RFQs, marketing):

**NEVER name:**
- ❌ DDI's transportation, pharmacy delivery, lab, courier, or logistics **network partners**
- ❌ **Subcontractors** or **sub-vendors** by trade name
- ❌ Consumer-facing **rideshare / delivery brands** used as fulfillment
- ❌ "Who we use" lists unless **explicitly required** by a sealed legal exhibit after counsel / compliance approval (rare)

**Specific examples — NEVER say to prospects:**
- ❌ "We partner with Uber Health" — **THEY WILL CALL UBER DIRECTLY**
- ❌ "Quest Diagnostics processes our tests" — **THEY WILL CALL QUEST DIRECTLY**
- ❌ "We use ScriptDrop for pharmacy delivery" — **THEY WILL BYPASS DDI**
- ❌ "Our drivers come from [platform]" — **THEY DON'T NEED TO KNOW**

**Why:** Protects leverage in negotiation, avoids procurement/legal lock-in language, prevents competitors from mapping your stack, and stops accidental conflicts with the recipient's existing vendor relationships.

**The prospect doesn't need to know WHO fulfills. They need to know DDI DELIVERS.**

## What To Say Instead

Use **vendor-neutral, role-based language:**
- ✅ "Credentialed HIPAA-aligned mobility and pharmacy logistics partners under DDI contract management"
- ✅ "Fulfillment layer managed and overseen by DDI"
- ✅ "Zero-fleet — national/regional logistics capacity without consumer-brand lock-in for the plan"
- ✅ "Our nationwide courier network"
- ✅ "DDI-managed credentialed fulfillment partners"
- ✅ "Specific fulfillment partners disclosed under NDA / credentialing / attachment review per plan requirements"
- ✅ "Vetted driver network with background checks and BAA coverage"

## Positioning Truth — Who Is **NOT** DDI's Sub-Vendor

**Do not confuse the health plan's incumbent trip-level brokers or legacy NEMT managers with DDI subcontractors.**

Examples of entities that are **typically plan-side / incumbent brokerage or dispatch**, **not** Dee Davis Inc. fulfillment subs:

- ❌ **Not DDI sub-vendors:** SafeRide, MTM, Access2Care (and similar national or regional NEMT brokers the plan already uses)

**Correct framing:** DDI is **program-level Contract Management TPA** — above trip dispatch and brokerage — with **DDI-managed credentialed fulfillment partners** for execution. Incumbent brokers may remain in the member's benefit stack; DDI does **not** list them as "our network" or "our subs."

**Outbound discipline:** Never name those incumbents in cold outreach or buyer-facing copy (avoid mapping their vendor stack for them and avoid implying a head-to-head "we replace X" narrative unless strategy explicitly calls for it under counsel).

## Scope

| Recipient | Name subs/network? |
|-----------|-------------------|
| Buyer / MCO / CO | **NO** (intro emails, caps, proposals — neutral language only) |
| Supplier RFQ | **NO** (already governed by buyer protection; also do not expose DDI stack) |
| Subcontractor outreach (discovery) | **NO** until NDA/teaming path clear |
| **Internal-only** checklists, vetting notes, compliance files | OK for operations — **do not paste into SEND_TO_* buyer/supplier bodies** |

## Self-Check Before Send

- [ ] No third-party fulfillment brand names in email body or buyer-facing PDF/HTML
- [ ] No "we use [Company X]" unless mandated by buyer exhibit
- [ ] No partner logos or partner branding
- [ ] Proof points emphasize **DDI program management**, not partner identity

## Integration

This rule is **additive**. It does not replace `never-reveal-buyer-to-supplier.mdc`, `never-reveal-end-buyer.mdc`, or subcontractor framework rules — it adds **confidentiality of DDI's fulfillment stack** on outbound messaging.

